CH = dict(num=4, tag="History", theme="Factories, workers, handicrafts and Indian industry under colonial rule",
 title="The Age of Industrialisation",
 intro="Industrialisation is often pictured as the story of big factories and steam engines. This chapter shows a more complex picture: hand production and small workshops lived alongside machines, and in colonial India industry grew under the shadow of British trade policy. It follows workers, weavers, merchants and advertisers.",
 big_questions=["What was proto-industrialisation?", "Why did hand labour survive alongside machines?", "How did industrialisation affect Indian weavers?", "How did Indian businessmen and factories develop?", "How did advertising and market-making work?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Before the Industrial Revolution"),
 ("p", "Even before factories, there was large-scale production for international markets in Europe. This is called **proto-industrialisation**: merchants gave work to peasants and artisans in the countryside who worked at home, so that the town merchant had a source of cheap labour."),
 ("ul", ["Merchants could not expand production in towns because **guilds** (associations of producers) controlled prices, training and entry. So they went to villages, where peasants with small plots needed extra income.", "A clothier supplied wool, collected the spun yarn and gave it to weavers, then to dyers and finishers before selling in the markets of London.", "Peasants and artisans accepted this system because of poverty, and families worked together, so land and loom both gave an income."]),
 ("h2", "Hand Labour and Steam Power"),
 ("ul", ["The first factories in England appeared in the **1730s**. **Cotton** was the leading sector in the 1780s; a series of inventions, including **Richard Arkwright's cotton mill**, speeded up carding, twisting and spinning. Mills allowed closer supervision and quality control.", "The steam engine, first devised by **Newcomen**, was improved by **James Watt** (patent 1781), who made it a source of power for industry. Cotton and iron and steel industries expanded; railways grew.", "Yet **industrialisation was slow**: even in 1914 the largest employment was in traditional industries like clothing and footwear, not factories. Seasonal industries (gas works, book binding, Christmas products) used hand labour. Many manufacturers preferred hand workers because of low wages, varying demand and the need for variety; aristocrats and the middle classes liked **hand-made goods**, which they felt were of better finish."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "In Victorian Britain there was no shortage of cheap labour. Machines needed high investment and repairs, so employers used machines mainly when wages were high or when production was large and steady."),
 ("h3", "Life of workers"),
 ("ul", ["Migrants from the countryside came to cities looking for jobs; jobs were seasonal and many workers waited in groups for work, relying on friends and relatives.", "Wages rose a little but real incomes were hit by rising prices; there were years of unemployment, and fear of new machines like the **spinning jenny** (introduced by James Hargreaves in 1764) led to attacks by workers, who feared that it would take their jobs."]),
 ("h2", "Industrialisation in the Colonies"),
 ("h3", "The decline of Indian textiles"),
 ("ul", ["Before machines, Indian silk and cotton goods dominated international markets. Merchants from **Surat, Hooghly and Masulipatnam** exported goods; the old ports declined and Bombay and Calcutta rose after the British gained control.", "From the late **eighteenth century** the East India Company's monopoly and the use of **gomastas** (paid servants) to supervise weavers, collect supplies and examine cloth reduced the weavers' freedom: they were forced to sell only to the company and take advances; clashes with the gomastas occurred.", "Cotton goods from **Manchester** flooded Indian markets in the nineteenth century because of the low duties; by the 1850s and 1860s, weavers had lost the export market and also faced competition at home. When the American Civil War (1861-65) cut off supplies of raw cotton, prices in India shot up and weavers could not afford yarn.", "By the early twentieth century, handloom weaving survived because of the **fly shuttle**, which increased productivity and brought lower costs. Fly shuttle was invented by John Kay in 1733. Weavers making coarse cloth held on better than those making fine cloth."]),
 ("h3", "Factories come up"),
 ("ul", ["The first **cotton mill in Bombay** was set up in **1854**; by 1862 four mills were working. The first **jute mill** was set up in Bengal in **1855**.", "**Elgin Mill** in Kanpur was set up in the 1860s, and the Ahmedabad mill in 1861. In 1874 the first spinning and weaving mill of Madras began production.", "Indian traders: Merchants such as **Dwarkanath Tagore** invested in shipping, mines, banking and plantations; **Parsi** families such as the Tatas, Petits and Jejeebhoys, and Marwari merchants like **Seth Hukumchand** (first Indian jute mill in Calcutta in 1917) and the Birlas grew rich in China trade, then invested in industry. **Jamsetji Tata** founded the Tata Iron and Steel Company (1907), whose plant at **Jamshedpur** began production in 1912.", "Many Indian merchants were barred from trade with Europe by the Company and were denied entry into shipping."]),
 ("h3", "Workers and the early factories"),
 ("p", "Most workers came from nearby districts and were peasants and artisans who found work in factories seasonally. Mill owners used a **jobber**, an old and trusted worker, to get new recruits, get them jobs, help them settle in the city and give them money in times of crisis. Jobbers became powerful and demanded money and favours."),
 ("h3", "Peculiarities of industrial growth"),
 ("ul", ["European **managing agencies** controlled a large part of Indian industry, including tea and coffee plantations, mining and jute; they invested their own and others' capital.", "From 1905, when the Swadeshi movement began, nationalists boycotted foreign cloth and mill production in India increased.", "The **First World War** brought huge demand for jute bags, army cloth, tents and boots, while Manchester supplies fell; Indian factories expanded and many new workers were employed. After the war, Manchester could not regain its colonial market, and Indian industry grew; by 1930s small-scale units (power looms, hand looms) formed much of industrial employment."]),
 ("h2", "Market for Goods"),
 ("ul", ["Producers create new consumers through **advertising**. Manchester cloth makers pasted labels on cloth bundles with the place of manufacture and the name ‘MADE IN MANCHESTER’; labels carried images of Indian gods and goddesses such as **Kartika, Lakshmi and Saraswati** to give the goods an appeal.", "From the late nineteenth century, calendars were printed to be hung in shops and homes; even people who could not read could see them. Images of national leaders and emperors appeared on them.", "When Indian manufacturers advertised, the message was clearly **nationalist**: ‘if you care for the nation, buy products that Indians produce.’"]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Britain", "Colonial India"], [["Early industry", "Cotton mills in 1780s; hand work remained", "Handloom decline; mills from 1854"], ["Power", "Steam engine (Watt)", "Imported machines and steam"], ["Labour", "Cheap labour reduced use of machines", "Jobbers recruited workers"], ["Market", "Domestic and colonial", "Manchester imports; Swadeshi"]]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What was proto-industrialisation?", "Large-scale rural production for merchants, before factories."),
  ("Why did hand labour survive in Britain?", "Low wages, seasonal demand and taste for hand-made goods."),
  ("Who was a gomasta?", "Paid servant of the East India Company who supervised weavers."),
  ("Who set up the first Indian cotton mill in Bombay and when?", "Cotton mill in Bombay, 1854 (founded by Indian businessmen led by Cowasjee Nanabhoy Davar)."),
  ("What did a jobber do?", "Recruited workers and controlled them in mills."),
  ("How did the First World War help Indian industry?", "Manchester supplies fell and war orders rose."),
  ("Why were Indian gods shown on labels?", "To give imported goods a familiar, respectable image."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Industry before factories grew through **proto-industrialisation** in the countryside.", "In Britain hand labour and machines co-existed for long; **steam power** and cotton led change.", "Indian weavers lost out to **Manchester goods** and to the control of **gomastas**.", "Mills arose in **Bombay (1854)** and Bengal; **Tata** set up steel at Jamshedpur in 1912.", "War and **Swadeshi** boosted Indian industry; advertisements built new markets."])
